window.SSS_CHAPTERS=(window.SSS_CHAPTERS||[]).concat([{"number":76,"title":"Chapter 76: Ekaksha [2]","paragraphs":["The silence between them stretched until the still water itself seemed to listen.","Ekaksha’s single eye remained fixed on Rael. When the rough voice returned, it carried something that might have been dry amusement.","\"The rabbit girl Mira.\"","Rael’s shoulders tensed before he could stop them. \"What about her?\"","\"You found her interesting didn’t you. Beautiful, even. Yet you took no step. Why?\"","The question landed with the weight of someone who had already watched the entire evening unfold. Rael tried to deflect the way he usually did when the conversation turned too close.","\"I didn’t thought of you as someone who give pep talk Ekaksha.\"","Ekaksha didn’t say anything and waited for Rael to explain better. Rael looked at him and thought he was not satisfied with the answer rael as just given an he is not letting the conversation go easily.","\"I don’t know maybe I was tired or that I haven’t done anything like that till now or maybe I just wanted to rest. Not everything has to be a grand decision.\"","\"You looked at her the way someone looks when they notice something for the first time.\" Ekaksha’s tone did not change. \"Then you pulled back. You kept the distance even when she offered less of it.\"","Rael let out a short, humorless breath. \"You’re teasing me. Either way she was just teasing and nothing would have happend anyway.\"","\"Am I wrong?\"","He opened his mouth to answer and found the easy reply missing. He had told himself the whole walk back to the room that Mira’s teasing was harmless, that she saw him as a child playing at adulthood, that responding would only encourage something neither of them meant. Those reasons still existed. They no longer felt complete. Underneath them sat a quieter, uglier hesitation he had not named until the chained figure forced it into the open.","Ekaksha spoke into the gap.","\"You think she will stop looking at you that same way once she knows that what you think of yourself as... Useless.\"","The words were plain. They cut anyway.","Rael’s jaw tightened. The First Realm, the public declaration of classless, the family’s cold dismissal, the way even a stranger’s casual kindness now arrived with an internal flinch—he had carried all of it into the inn without examining the shape it made. Hearing it spoken aloud in this place made the flinch harder to ignore. For him it has become harder to connect with anyone.","For a time he said nothing. The water under him remained almost perfectly still. The seven chains held their eternal tension.","Then, quieter:","\"You’re not useless, Rael. Not to me atleast.\"","The statement came without softness, yet it carried more force than any of the earlier probes. Rael felt something in his chest shift—not healed, not resolved, only acknowledged by a presence that had known him longer than any living relative. He stared at the faceless head and the single pale eye, and the questions he had been holding began to surface in a different order.","\"How do you know about her?\" he asked. \"About Mira. About what I was thinking when she leaned over the table. You told me before that you couldn’t see what I see.\"","Ekaksha did not look away.","\"Have I said that?\"","Rael waited.","\"You and I are one Rael. Remember that.\"","The sentence settled over the water like a change in pressure. Rael had always treated the visits as visitations—something that entered his sleep from elsewhere or rose out of a sealed corner of his own mind. The idea that the boundary was thinner than that, that the figure in the chains shared the same continuous thread of experience, rearranged every previous conversation in retrospect. The help with studies. The quiet presence through childhood. The year of absence after the awakening. All of it sat differently if \"one\" was not a metaphor.","He did not ask for proof. The fact that Ekaksha could speak of Mira’s silver hair and the exact temperature of the embarrassment in the stairwell was already proof enough.","Rael’s next question came out slower, more careful.","\"Was it you?\"","\"Was what me?\"","\"The seal on my Intelligence. The thing that keeps it locked at the bottom of the status screen. Did you put it there?\"","\"No.\"","The answer was immediate and flat.","\"Whoever did that does not mean you well and I only want to see you rise.\"","Rael absorbed the words. An enemy capable of reaching into the foundation of his attributes before he even awakened, of leaving a mark that still resisted every ordinary attempt to understand it. The list of people with that kind of reach was short and dangerous. He filed the knowledge beside the other unfinished debts he carried.","\"What do you want?\" Rael asked at last. \"From me. From this. You’ve been here my whole life. You help when you feel like it, you stay silent when you don’t, and you still won’t say what any of it is for. What do you want?\"","Ekaksha’s formed lips moved in the faintest suggestion of a smile.","\"I already have what I want.\"","The reply gave nothing and closed the subject at the same time. Rael felt the familiar frustration rise and then subside. Some answers still could not be forced. The figure in the chains had never pretended otherwise.","A sound interrupted the stillness—a single, sharp note of metal under strain.","Rael’s attention snapped to the chains. One of them, the heavy link that had circled Ekaksha’s neck, had begun to loosen. The iron looked older than the others, the surface pitted, the tension no longer absolute. As he watched, the final holding strength failed. The chain slipped free of the pale throat and swung outward in a sudden, heavy arc, directly toward him.","Rael threw himself sideways.","He was almost fast enough.","The end of the chain brushed across his open palm as it passed. Pain flared—white, immediate, and wrong in a way ordinary burns were not. He hit the water hard, sending a wide ring of ripples outward, and clutched the hand to his chest. The chain continued its swing and then simply stopped, hanging slack in the air as if the force that had animated it had been spent.","Ekaksha remained suspended by the remaining six chains, the newly freed length of iron dangling uselessly beside him. The single eye watched Rael without visible concern.","\"I can finally move my head.\"","Rael didn’t know what to say this was something that never happened before it was the first time he was seeing any changes in the chains.","\"You should wake up boy,\" the rough voice said.","The water, the sky, and the chained figure began to lose solidity at the edges. Rael tried to speak and found the dream already thinning around the words. The last thing he felt was the continuing burn in his palm, too precise and too deep to belong to ordinary sleep.","He woke in the inn bed with a sharp inhale.","Morning light—Aetherion’s long morning—pressed against the window it was still very dim light. The room was quiet. His body ached in the familiar places from the previous day’s fights. He sat up and opened his right hand.","Across the palm ran a clean, raised scar in the exact pattern of a heavy chain link. The skin around it was pink and tender, the mark already set as if it had been there for days rather than minutes. He closed the hand, opened it again, and felt the phantom heat still lingering under the surface.","Rael stared at the scar for a long time.","\"So it wasn’t just my imagination after all.\"","---"]},{"number":77,"title":"Chapter 77: First Friend In New World","paragraphs":["Rael sat on the edge of the bed with his right hand open under the mana-lamp light. The chain-shaped scar cut cleanly across the palm, raised and still faintly warm, as if the iron had left a memory under the skin. He turned the hand once, then again, and tried to make the pieces fit.","Why had that particular chain come loose? The others had held for as long as he had been visiting that same place for all his life. The neck chain suddenly gone loose did not explain the sudden failure or the way it had swung toward him with purpose. And how could he have seen Ekaksha inside his sleep for an entire life without ever learning what the entity actually was? The questions circled without landing. One conclusion refused to move: no one—human, entity, or otherwise—offered help for years without some form of return. Ekaksha had guided him through childhood lessons, had steadied him in ways Rael still could not fully map, and had asked for nothing obvious in exchange. That absence of visible price made the relationship more confusing, not less. He did not hate the chained figure. He simply no longer trusted the shape of the kindness.","Outside the window the light was thin and blue-gray. Aetherion’s long night was ending; the first of the twin suns had not yet cleared the walls, but the darkness had begun to lift. Early enough that the city still felt half-asleep.","He uncorked one of the minor health potions that he bought from the market before from his storage ring and poured the pale liquid over the scar. The potion sank in. The pain did not change. He waited longer than necessary, then tried a second small application. Nothing. The burn sat outside the reach of ordinary recovery the same way. In the First Realm he had burned his whole arm till black and watched lesser potions close the damage in minutes. This was different—deeper, cleaner, and stubborn. He would need a higher-tier potion or an actual healer. For now he wrapped the palm in clean bandage, tight enough to protect it, loose enough that he could still close his fingers.","Dressing took longer than usual. Every flex of the hand sent a bright, precise spike up his wrist. He gritted his teeth, finished with the straps of his armor, and went downstairs.","The common room was empty. Chairs stood on tables. The hearth held only ash. The air still carried a faint trace of last night’s cooking, but the space itself felt paused. From the kitchen came the soft sounds of someone already working—water, a knife on a board, the low clink of pottery. Footsteps approached the swinging door. Mira pushed through with a cloth over one shoulder and her silver hair tied back more practically than the night before. The bunny ears flicked once when she saw him.","\"Well, well. The serious customer rises with the suns.\" The teasing note was already in her voice. \"Couldn’t sleep, or did you just miss my face?\"","Rael gave a small, tired sound that might have been a laugh. \"Something like that.\"","She started to say more, then her eyes dropped to the white bandage around his right hand. The playful tilt of her head changed.","\"What happened?\"","\"Burned it. Turning off the lamp.\"","Mira’s ears flattened slightly. \"Don’t lie. Our lamps are made of mana-stone. They don’t burn skin.\" She waited. When he offered nothing else she exhaled and nodded once, accepting the silence without pushing. \"All right. Don’t tell me. Sit down anyway. I can help.\"","He sat. She pulled a chair close and held out her own hand, palm up. After a moment he rested his bandaged hand in hers. Her fingers were careful as they unwrapped the cloth. When the chain scar came into view she went still.","\"That’s not a normal burn.\"","\"I noticed.\"","Mira closed her eyes. A soft, cool light gathered around her hands—clean and steady, the unmistakable signature of a healing class. She worked in silence for a time, brows drawn together. The light intensified. Sweat began to show at her temple.","\"You’re a healer,\" Rael said quietly.","\"Among other things.\" She did not look up. \"Don’t worry about payment. I don’t charge for this. The inn already keeps me fed.\"","He watched the stubborn scar slowly lose its angry color under her effort. \"You’re rabbit-folk. In a major human city. And a high-end healer at that. How does that work here?\"","Mira’s mouth quirked, though her focus stayed on the hand. \"Ashfall does a lot of business with mixed-blood communities. Rabbit-folk, fox-kin, a few others. As long as you pay taxes and don’t start trouble, most people only care whether you can do the job. A healer who can close bad wounds is welcome almost anywhere.\" She guided a fresh pulse of light into the deepest part of the mark and added, lighter, \"The ears are just a bonus for the customers.\"","He noticed she had steered the conversation away from the rarity of her class. He did not press.","\"This burn is fighting me,\" she murmured after another stretch of silence. \"Whatever made it wasn’t ordinary fire or acid. I’m having to push harder than I expected.\"","\"You don’t have to—\"","\"I said I’d help.\" The words were firm but not unkind. She poured more of her skill into the work until the last of the raised chain pattern smoothed into ordinary pink skin. When she finally released his hand she sat back and wiped her forehead with the cloth from her shoulder. The cool light faded.","\"There. Try not to collect any more mystery burns for a few days.\"","The teasing tone returned, but it sat thinner than before. Something quieter moved underneath it—tiredness, or an old sadness she had not intended to show. Rael felt the difference the way he had felt the difference between ordinary wolves and the Alpha. He adjusted the way he spoke to match.","\"Thank you. Really.\"","Mira blinked, then smiled with less performance in it. \"You’re welcome, Rael.\"","They sat in the empty common room while the light outside strengthened. Conversation drifted to safer ground. The earlier heat and awkwardness from the previous evening did not return. In its place settled a simpler ease. Rael found himself answering without measuring every word. Mira, for her part, stopped reaching for the sharpest tease and simply talked. He felt like he made a first friend in Second Realm.","When the first real gold of the twin suns crossed the windowsill he stood. She stood with him. On impulse—practical, necessary, and only slightly dishonest—he reached out and closed his hand briefly around hers.","[Class detected]","[Class: Healer (A-Rank)]","[Do you want to copy?]","[Y/N]","The system acknowledgment flickered at the edge of his awareness. He accepted it in the same heartbeat and let go.","Mira looked at their hands, then at his face, ears flicking in clear surprise and with a blush. \"What was that for?\"","\"Thank you for everything Mira.\" Rael said again, and meant it on more than one level.","After sometime he left her in the quiet common room with the morning light and the faint scent of bread beginning to rise from the kitchen, the new class sitting cleanly beside the others, and the chain scar gone as if it had never been more than a dream. Outside, Ashfall was waking. He had cores to sell, a week already paid for, and a long list of questions that still had no answers. For the moment it was enough to walk out of the inn with both hands steady and as for Ekaksha he can’t know anything about him now so he just stopped thinking for now.","---"]},{"number":78,"title":"Chapter 78: Adventurer Guild [1]","paragraphs":["Rael turned his right hand over in the morning light and studied the place where the chain scar had been. The skin was smooth now, faintly pink, but the memory of the pain remained. Ordinary minor health potions had done nothing. Mira had needed to push her skill harder than she expected just to close it. Whatever mark Ekaksha’s loosened chain had left was not a normal burn, and that fact sat uncomfortably at the edge of his thoughts. He set it aside for later and opened the new class window instead.","[Healer (A-Rank)] (30 days remaining)","Description:","A powerful support class specializing in healing wounds, restoring vitality, and cleansing harmful effects. An A-Rank Healer can save lives even after severe injuries.","Skills:","[Greater Heal] (Novice) — Channels divine energy to rapidly heal severe wounds, restore damaged tissue and limbs, and recover a large amount of vitality.","[Purification] (Novice) — Removes poisons, curses, diseases, and other harmful effects from the target. The effectiveness depends on the strength of the affliction.","The value of the healer class was obvious. Rael fought by spending his body. He took hits to create openings, pushed skills until his mana ran thin, and walked out of every serious fight with new cuts and bruises. Until now recovery had meant potions, rest, or simply enduring. Greater Heal changed that equation. Even at Novice mastery it offered a way to restore himself between encounters instead of limping back to the walls every time he overreached. Purification was a quieter insurance policy against the kinds of lingering toxins and curses the deeper Wildlands were said to carry. He had wanted a healing option for weeks. Finding an A-Rank version on a waitress in a mid-tier city inn had never been part of the plan.","That thought circled back to Mira herself. Ashfall was large and prosperous by the standards of the outer territories, yet it was still far from the central human strongholds where high-rank specialists usually gathered. An A-Rank Healer could have commanded serious fees in any major capital. Instead she wore a shortened maid uniform, teased customers, and refused payment for the work she had done on his hand. She had sidestepped every question about her history with practiced ease. Rael did not push. He carried enough secrets of his own—the true nature of Analyze, the bloodline, the entity in his dreams, the sealed Intelligence—to recognize the same careful silence in someone else. Whatever reasons kept her in Ashfall were hers. He was simply grateful the chance encounter had given him the class.","He decided not to leave the walls today. The Fang Alpha and the pack had left him with cores, usable remains, and a clear need to turn them into coin or materials before he pushed farther out. Learning the city’s layout and rhythms mattered as much as another hunt. He left the inn and joined the streets as they woke.","Ashfall in the early hours felt different from the same streets at dusk. Mana-stone lamps still glowed in the narrower lanes, but their light was fading against the rising gold of the twin suns. Shutters opened in uneven sequence. A blacksmith in a thick leather apron rolled a cart of raw bars toward a forge already leaking heat into the alley; the ring of an early hammer started up before Rael had passed the next corner. Merchants unlocked heavy doors and kicked wooden wedges under them to hold them wide, calling quiet greetings to neighbors while they set out the first trays of goods. The smell of baking bread mixed with coal smoke and the faint metallic tang of the smithies.","Farther along the avenue he saw hunters moving in the opposite direction—toward the gates. Some walked alone, still buckling straps. Others moved in small knots, talking in low voices about routes and meeting points. They wanted the early hours outside the walls, when the most accessible hunting grounds had not yet been picked over by the day’s traffic. Rael watched them go and felt no urge to follow. His body still carried the previous day’s fatigue, and the cores in his storage ring would not sell themselves.","Near a wider plaza he passed a group of young people in matching dark-blue uniforms. They carried practice swords in plain scabbards and walked with the straight-backed discipline of students heading to morning drills. Sword-art schools. The sight pulled a familiar thread of thought loose.","There were two broad ways an awakener fought.","The first used mana inward. Fighters classes circulated power through muscle, bone, and tendon, hardening the body itself until fists, blades, or arrows struck with force far beyond ordinary flesh. Swordsmen, close-combat specialists, many archers, and most tanks belonged to this path. Their mana rarely left the skin except as reinforcement.","The second path sent mana outward. Mages classes shaped fire, ice, lightning, or other attributes into projectiles, barriers, and zones of control. Healers did the same with restorative energy. The body remained relatively ordinary; the power lived in what they released into the world.","Rael’s own situation sat awkwardly between the two. Infernomancer and Frostheart both expressed themselves as external skills—bolts, walls, armor of ice, fields of flame. Analyze did not care which style he used; it only cared that he understood what he copied. He had trained in the Blackthorn sword forms since childhood, yet those forms were built around dark-attribute reinforcement that traveled through the blade and the body together. Without that attribute the techniques felt incomplete, like trying to swing a tool whose weight had changed. He could still use the footwork and the angles. The full art remained closed to him for now. One day he might find a way to bridge the gap. Until then he needed either a different sword style that matched the power he actually carried, or enough raw skill to make the incomplete version lethal on its own. The problem was not urgent today, but it would not wait forever.","After more than half an hour of steady walking the Adventurer Guild rose ahead of him.","The building stood near the City Lord’s tower, large and practical rather than beautiful, with wide doors that stayed open from early morning until well after the twin suns set. Rael stepped inside and stopped just past the threshold.","The main hall was already crowded. Parties clustered around notice boards thick with handwritten and crystal-posted quests. Solo hunters waited in lines at the appraisal and sales counters. Mages in marked robes argued quietly over the price of rare materials. Mixed-blood beast-kin—wolf-kin, fox-kin, a few with heavier feline traits—moved through the crowd without drawing special attention. Relations between humans and beast-kin had been stable on Titan for generations; here in Aetherion the same practical tolerance seemed to hold. What struck Rael hardest was the pressure. Dozens of stronger auras overlapped in the enclosed space. A person with no combat experience might have found it hard to breathe. Even he, freshly leveled and carrying two combat classes, felt the weight of how many people in the room could kill him without particular effort. Most of them never glanced his way. He was simply another young awakener with a storage ring and something to sell.","The Adventurer Guild itself functioned like a public company open to anyone who could pay the modest registration fee. Once inside, an awakener could accept posted quests, sell monster materials and cores at the guild’s posted rates, or request basic appraisal. The system was impersonal and efficient. It existed so that solo hunters and small parties without noble backing still had a place to turn their kills into coin and information. Private guilds also existed—many of them founded and funded by noble houses—but those operated more like closed companies. They kept the best contracts, the most profitable hunting rights, and the cleaner political connections for their own members. To most noble-born awakeners the public Adventurer Guild was a necessary utility for commoners and outsiders, not a place they preferred to stand in for long.","Rael had no noble backing left to him and no private guild waiting to claim his kills. The public hall would do. He adjusted the fit of his storage ring, picked the shortest line that looked like it handled materials rather than quest registration, and waited his turn among the noise, the overlapping auras, and the low constant talk of prices, routes, and yesterday’s close calls. The city was still teaching him its shape. Today he would learn how it paid.","---","Thank you for reading this far.","Rael’s path has only just begun. Your time and attention mean more than you know. Every Chapter exists because someone is still turning the page.","There is much left ahead: stronger foes, deeper secrets, and the long road toward the strength he is still chasing. From now on the story will start to follow new arcs no longer just hunting and shopping a real stories. I hope you will continue walking it with him.","Thank you again for being here.","See you in the next Chapter."]},{"number":79,"title":"Chapter 79: Adventurer Guild [2]","paragraphs":["Rael found the materials counter along the side of the main hall. An old man with short white hair sat behind it, quietly sorting a stack of documents with the unhurried rhythm of someone who had performed the same motions for decades. The noise of the guild—parties arguing over quest rankings, the scrape of boots, the low hum of overlapping auras—seemed to stop just short of his desk, as if even the building respected the small island of calm he kept there.","Rael stepped up and set the edge of his storage ring against the counter.","\"Can I help you, young man?\"","\"I want to sell some monster corpses.\"","The old man’s hand paused halfway through turning a page. He glanced up, took in Rael’s age, the still-new look of his armor, and the absence of any guild badge.","\"Monster corpses, huh?\"","\"Yes.\"","He leaned back in his chair and studied Rael for a longer moment. \"Are you an adventurer?\"","\"Not yet.\"","\"Not yet?\"","\"This is my first time coming to the Adventurer Guild. I don’t have a guild card either.\"","The old man’s eyebrows rose, then settled. A small, almost private smile touched the corner of his mouth. \"First time. All right. We’ll handle registration after. First I need to see what you’ve brought.\" He closed the ledger, pushed himself to his feet with a soft grunt, and nodded toward a wide door at the back of the hall. \"Follow me.\"","They left the main floor together. The roar of voices faded behind the heavy wood until only their footsteps remained. The storage and appraisal wing smelled of preservative herbs, cold stone, and the faint metallic scent of old blood that never fully left the air. Crates and tagged remains lined the walls in neat rows. The old man stopped in the center of an open inspection space and folded his arms.","\"Go ahead. Let’s see what a first-timer brings in.\"","Rael nodded and reached for the storage ring.","A heavy, wet thud shook the floor. The Venom Spider’s corpse unfolded across the stones, legs still half-curled, carapace scorched and split in places from fire and ice. The old man’s eyes widened a fraction. He stepped closer, crouched, and ran a practiced gaze over the size, the wound patterns, the residual traces of venom sacs.","\"Venom Spider. Level sixteen or seventeen, by the look of it.\" He stroked his short white beard, the initial surprise already turning into professional appraisal. \"Tricky things. The big ones especially. Webs, poison, and they think while they hunt. Not bad for a first hunt, kid. Not bad at all.\"","He straightened and gave Rael a solid pat on the shoulder. The gesture carried real warmth. In more than thirty years behind this counter he had watched countless new awakeners walk through the guild doors with shining eyes and shaking hands. Most brought one corpse. The lucky or the careful brought two or three. Almost all of them treated that first sale like a ceremony, even when the coin was modest. Seeing them take the step—leaving the safety of the walls, killing something that wanted them dead, and returning to turn it into a future—was the part of the job he had never grown tired of.","He had not always stood behind a counter.","Once he had been an adventurer himself, younger and hungrier, running the outer routes with a small party that felt invincible until the day it was not. A nested dungeon that should have been routine turned into a collapse of tunnels and a tide of things that did not care about ranks or plans. He still woke sometimes with the memory of the dark and the weight of stone and the sound of someone he could not reach. When he crawled out, he put the sword away. Fear had done what no lecture could: it had shown him the exact shape of his own limit. The guild offered quieter work—appraisal, intake, the steady buying of what other people risked their lives to bring back. He took the post and never left. Thirty years later he still felt a clean, uncomplicated happiness when a new face appeared with their first real kills. It meant the wheel kept turning. Someone else was beginning the same road he had stepped off.","\"You’ve got a promising future ahead of you,\" he said, and meant it.","Rael met his eyes without excitement or false modesty. \"I have more.\"","The old man’s smile froze in place. \"...More?\"","Rael touched the ring again.","A Fang Wolf hit the floor. Then another. Then a third. He did not rush, but he did not pause either. Corpses appeared in a steady sequence—dark fur, oversized fangs, the varied wounds of blade, fire, and ice. Five. Ten. More than a dozen. The inspection space filled until the old man had to step back to keep his boots clear. The pile became a low mound of Savage-tier remains, enough material to keep the guild’s lower-tier supply lines fed for days.","The old man’s mouth opened and then closed. He stared at the growing heap as if waiting for the joke’s punchline. When Rael finally stopped, the silence in the room was thick.","\"How...?\"","\"How what?\"","The old man pointed at the corpses with a hand that was no longer entirely steady. \"How did you get all of these?\"","\"I hunted them.\"","\"By yourself?\"","\"Yes.\"","Something complicated passed across the old man’s face—professional assessment wrestling with simple disbelief. Fang Wolf packs were not legendary threats, but they were coordinated, fast, and unforgiving of mistakes. Experienced parties treated them with respect. Solo hunters who underestimated the pack often became the next meal the survivors shared. Yet the evidence lay in front of him in plain, cooling heaps.","He rubbed his temples. \"Wait. Don’t tell me you took all of these alone.\"","\"I did.\"","The old man stared at him for several long seconds, searching for the crack in the story, the missing party members, the hired help waiting outside. He found only a calm young man standing amid enough death to stock a butcher’s month.","Rael reached for the ring one last time. \"There’s still one more.\"","\"One more?\"","The Alpha Fang Wolf hit the stones like a falling beam. The sound was deeper than the others, heavier. Even in death the body dominated the space—larger frame, darker fur, the long killing fangs that marked it as the leader of the pack. The air in the room seemed to thin.","The old man took one step forward, then another. He crouched beside the corpse and examined it with the careful attention of a man hoping the evidence would change if he looked harder. The wounds told a clear story: concentrated fire, ice, precise blade work, and a final decisive cut. No secondary weapon signatures. No signs of a prolonged group struggle.","\"...This is an Alpha Fang Wolf.\"","\"Yes.\"","He stood slowly. \"You killed this?\"","\"Yes.\"","\"Where?\"","\"In the Wildlands.\"","\"I know it’s in the Wildlands!\" The words came out sharper than he intended. He reined them in at once. \"I mean—did you do this with a party?\"","\"No.\"","\"Then who helped you?\"","\"No one.\"","Silence settled over the storage room. The old man looked from the Alpha to the mound of ordinary Fang Wolves to the scorched Venom Spider and finally back to Rael. In thirty years he had seen talented first-timers. He had seen lucky first-timers. He had never seen a first-timer walk in with a solo Alpha kill and enough pack corpses to carpet the floor.","For a moment the weight of that fact pressed the air out of the room. Then the absurdity of it—the calm boy, the mountain of proof, the complete mismatch between expectation and reality—broke something loose in the old man’s chest. He started to laugh. It began as a disbelieving huff and grew until he had to wipe the corner of his eye.","\"Hahaha! Hahahaha!\"","Rael blinked, waiting.","The old man caught his breath and gestured at the Alpha with an open hand. \"A new awakener walks into my guild for the first time, says he wants to sell some monsters, and then pulls out dozens of corpses—and the boss on top of them!\" He shook his head, still laughing. \"This has to be a joke.\"","\"A joke?\"","\"I know! It’s real and that’s what makes it worse!\"","He finally straightened, composure returning in uneven pieces. The laughter faded into a quieter, more complex expression—amusement, residual shock, and the same genuine warmth he reserved for every new hunter who crossed his counter, only magnified by the scale of what he had just witnessed. These monsters were not the stuff of legends. Experienced adventurers killed Fang Wolves and Venom Spiders without writing songs about it. That was not the point. The point was the boy standing in front of him had done it alone, on his first real day beyond the walls, and had walked back in carrying the proof like it was simply the natural result of the work.","The old man looked at Rael with a mixture of disbelief and something brighter.","\"Kid...\" He paused, searching for words that could hold both the years behind the counter and the evidence on the floor. \"Just what kind of first hunt did you have?\"","---"]}]);
